# AWPTCM-T33369 (210) Authentication&Security_TwoStepAuthentication - Mac-based 1st then 802.1x 2nd
# Working log, run 1 -- tb277 / swi_d (AT-x960-56YSQ) -- group B, authmodes-2026-10-23T1304
# Tester: bench-runner subagent, 2026-10-23 15:57-16:09 NZDT (02:57-03:09 UTC)

## What the case asks, and how it was measured

No authored steps in `ck.db`. TPS row **208 "Mac-based (1st) then 802.1x (2nd)" reads `Yes`**.
The dispatch set the objective: *the point is the ORDER -- MAC auth must succeed first, and only
then is 802.1X attempted. Show that a client that passes MAC but fails 802.1X is not authorised,
and one that fails MAC never reaches 802.1X at all. Both halves on the wire.*

Both halves are below, on the wire, plus a third leg the dispatch did not ask for and which
turns out to be the cleanest proof of all: **reversing the configured order with
`auth two-step order dot1x auth-mac` and watching the SAME MAC with the SAME credentials change
from 1 EAPOL frame to 8.**

Graded on frames throughout -- EAPOL frame tables (`tools/eapol.py`) and ARP request/reply counts
(`tools/arpleg.py`). `show` and the DUT's log are second and third witnesses only.

