## STEP 0 -- the OFF baseline: with two-step DISABLED, MAC authentication ALONE authorises

`auth-mac enable` + `dot1x port-control auto` on the port, `twoStepAuthentication: configured:
disabled`.

```
LEG T33369-S0-twostep-OFF-macauth-alone  (MAC C, 4 x ARP)
  cap: 7 frame(s)
    x4  ARP-REQ   who-has 10.38.22.66  tell 10.38.22.72  [eth 02:0d:b9:00:33:69 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
    x3  ARP-REPLY 10.38.22.66 is-at e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43    [eth e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 02:0d:b9:00:33:69]

show auth supplicant interface port1.0.48
  totalSupplicantNum: 1   authorizedSupplicantNum: 1
  Supplicant name: 02-0d-b9-00-33-69
  Supplicant address: 020d.b900.3369
    authenticationMethod: MAC-based Authentication
    portStatus: Authorized - currentId: 1
```

**STEP 0 verdict: PASS.** The port is in the ordinary either-or mode: **MAC authentication on its
own is enough**, the supplicant is `Authorized`, named after its own MAC, and the dataplane
passes (3 of 4; the first request arrives while the entry is still being created). This is the
number the two-step legs have to change, and it is changed by **one command**.

## STEP 1 -- two-step ON, order `auth-mac dot1x`: MAC alone is no longer enough

```
configure terminal / interface port1.0.48
 auth two-step enable
 auth two-step order auth-mac dot1x
end

show running-config interface port1.0.48
 ...
 auth-mac enable
 dot1x port-control auto
 auth two-step enable
 auth two-step order auth-mac dot1x

show auth interface port1.0.48
  authMac: enabled
  dot1x: enabled
  twoStepAuthentication:
    configured: enabled
    actual: enabled
    order: mac dot1x
```

### STEP 1a -- the same MAC, the same frames, the opposite result

```
LEG T33369-S1a-macauth-only-under-twostep  (MAC C, 4 x ARP)
  cap: 4 frame(s)
    x4  ARP-REQ who-has 10.38.22.66 tell 10.38.22.72  [eth 02:0d:b9:00:33:69 -> ff:ff:ff:ff:ff:ff]
  ARP replies: 0

show auth supplicant interface port1.0.48
  totalSupplicantNum: 1   authorizedSupplicantNum: 0
  Supplicant name: -
  Supplicant address: 020d.b900.3369
    authenticationMethod: 802.1X                 <-- step 1 passed; the DUT is now on step 2
    portStatus: Unauthorized - currentId: 1
    PAE: state: Connecting - portMode: Auto      <-- waiting for 802.1X
```

**STEP 1a verdict: PASS, and it is the A/B the case rests on.** Nothing changed between STEP 0
and STEP 1a but `auth two-step enable`: the same MAC sending the same four ARP requests went from
**3 replies and `Authorized`** to **0 replies and `Unauthorized`**. And the DUT says why in its
own fields: the supplicant's `authenticationMethod` has flipped from `MAC-based Authentication`
to **`802.1X`** and the PAE state is **`Connecting`** -- the first step passed and the switch is
now waiting for the second.

The DUT's log names the step explicitly:
`802.1X[1094]: port1.0.48: Two-Step Authentication(1st step: MAC) successful for
02-0d-b9-00-33-69, Mac 020d.b900.3369`.

