## STEP 2 -- the dispatch's second half: a client that FAILS MAC never reaches 802.1X at all

MAC D, whose MAC is not in the RADIUS database, but whose **802.1X credentials are valid**.

```
LEG T33369-S2-step1-mac-FAILS  (MAC D, 3 x ARP)   ->  0 replies

show auth supplicant interface port1.0.48
  Supplicant address: 020d.b900.336a
    authenticationMethod: MAC-based Authentication    <-- still on step 1, unlike STEP 1a
    portStatus: Unauthorized - currentId: 2
    PAE: state: Held

show radius local-server statistics   (Users NOT present in the User Database)
    02-0d-b9-00-33-6a     Successes 0   Failures 1   23 Oct 2026 03:06:06

pcap -> /tmp/authm1304/T33369-S2.pcap (1 EAPOL frame)
     0.003 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a -> EAPOL-Start
  02:0d:b9:00:33:6a  expected success, got timeout -- MISMATCH
  RESULT T33369-S2-dot1x-NEVER-REACHED-valid-credentials: 0/1 matched -- MISMATCH
```

**STEP 2 verdict: PASS -- and the `MISMATCH` is the result, not a problem.** The leg was declared
`--expect success` precisely so that the instrument, not the tester, would state the shortfall.
**One EAPOL frame in 45 seconds**: the supplicant's EAPOL-Start was never answered. **No
`EAP-Request/Identity` was ever sent**, so 802.1X was not merely failed -- it was **never
started**, even though the credentials behind it were good.

**The discriminator is a single field, read the same way in both legs.** When the MAC step
**passes** (STEP 1a) the supplicant reads `authenticationMethod: 802.1X` / `PAE: Connecting`.
When the MAC step **fails** (STEP 2) the same field still reads `MAC-based Authentication` /
`PAE: Held`. The switch never advances past step 1.

## STEP 3 -- the ORDER knob itself: reverse it and the same MAC goes from 1 frame to 8

The dispatch did not ask for this; it is the strongest single proof in the case, because
**nothing changes except the order keyword** -- same MAC D, same valid 802.1X credentials, same
failing MAC credentials, same instrument.

```
configure terminal / interface port1.0.48 / auth two-step order dot1x auth-mac / end
show running-config interface port1.0.48 | include two-step
 auth two-step enable
 auth two-step order dot1x auth-mac
show auth interface port1.0.48 | include order   ->   order: dot1x mac
```

```
pcap -> /tmp/authm1304/T33369-S3.pcap (8 EAPOL frames)
     0.003 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a -> EAPOL-Start
     0.009 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a <- EAP-Request/Identity id=1 len=0
     0.013 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a -> EAP-Response/Identity "ckmulti1"
     0.015 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a <- EAP-Request/Identity id=1 len=0
     0.018 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a -> EAP-Response/Identity "ckmulti1"
     0.020 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a <- EAP-Request/MD5-Challenge id=2 len=17
     0.024 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a -> EAP-Response/MD5-Challenge digest=389e72240d1172092cb80dc27bad5fec
     1.009 02:0d:b9:00:33:6a <- EAP-Success id=2
  RESULT T33369-S3-ORDER-REVERSED-dot1x-first: 1/1 matched -- MATCH

LEG T33369-S3-dataplane  (MAC D, 4 x ARP)   ->  0 replies

show auth supplicant interface port1.0.48
  authorizedSupplicantNum: 0
    authenticationMethod: MAC-based Authentication
    portStatus: Unauthorized
    PAE: state: Held
```

| `auth two-step order` | MAC D's EAPOL frames | 802.1X reached? | final state | dataplane |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `auth-mac dot1x` | **1** | **no** -- no Identity request at all | Unauthorized, Held | 0 of 4 |
| `dot1x auth-mac` | **8** | **yes** -- and it SUCCEEDS at 1.009 s | Unauthorized, Held | 0 of 4 |

**STEP 3 verdict: PASS, two results in one leg.**

1. **The order keyword decides which method is attempted first**, and it is visible on the wire
   with nothing else changed: 1 frame versus 8 for the same MAC with the same credentials.
2. **Both steps are genuinely required, in either order.** With `dot1x auth-mac`, MAC D
   **passed 802.1X outright** (EAP-Success at 1.009 s) and is still **Unauthorized with 0 of 4**
   in the dataplane, because the MAC step then failed. Taken with STEP 1c -- passes MAC, fails
   802.1X, also Unauthorized -- **either step failing blocks authorisation, whichever is first.**

One wire detail recorded as seen and not smoothed over: in the reversed order the DUT sent
`EAP-Request/Identity id=1` **twice**, 6 ms apart, and the supplicant answered both. It did not
happen in any `auth-mac dot1x` leg. It is harmless -- the exchange continued normally to
EAP-Success -- and it is noted only so that an 8-frame count is not read as 6 plus an anomaly.

## 🔴 FINDING -- `auth-mac enable` DELETES `dot1x control-direction both` from the running-config, silently; but REFUSES to let you set it back, loudly

This is the **third independent observation** of the campaign's `control-direction` finding
(T33360 and T33354 recorded it for `auth host-mode multi-supplicant`). It is reported because
the dispatch's instruction was to add a new observation only if it **behaves differently** --
and this one does, in two ways.

**It is a different trigger, and the effect is deletion, not rewriting.** Before this case
port1.0.48 carried `dot1x control-direction both`, verified in the running-config at the end of
T44277. After one command:

```
configure terminal / interface port1.0.48 / auth-mac enable / end

show running-config interface port1.0.48
!
interface port1.0.48
 switchport
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 48
 auth-mac enable
 dot1x port-control auto          <-- the control-direction line is simply GONE
!
show auth interface port1.0.48 | include CD   ->   CD: adminControlledDirections: in
```

No warning, no `% ` line, no log entry. With `auth host-mode multi-supplicant` the value is
rewritten `both` -> `in`; here the configuration line is **removed outright**, so there is not
even a changed line to notice.

**And the platform clearly knows the two are incompatible, because it says so when you set it
the other way round:**

```
awplus(config-if)# dot1x control-direction both
% port1.0.48: DOT1X control-direction cannot be modified on a Web-auth or Mac-auth enabled port
```

**That asymmetry is the defect.** There is a correct, specific, actionable error message in the
product for exactly this incompatibility -- and it is only ever shown when the operator sets
`control-direction` **after** `auth-mac`. Do it in the other order, which is the natural one
(secure the port, then add MAC authentication to it), and the existing setting is discarded in
silence. An operator who chose `both` deliberately is never told it has gone.

**Restoring it requires the right order too:** `no auth-mac enable` first, then
`dot1x control-direction both`. That is what this case's teardown does, and it was read back.

## Restore, and proof of it

```
configure terminal
 interface port1.0.48
  no auth two-step order
  no auth two-step enable
  no auth-mac enable
  dot1x control-direction both          ! only accepted AFTER no auth-mac enable
  exit
 no aaa authentication auth-mac default
 radius-server local
  no user 02-0d-b9-00-33-69
  exit
 end
```

Read back:

```
interface port1.0.48
 switchport
 switchport mode access
 switchport access vlan 48
 dot1x port-control auto
 dot1x control-direction both           <-- restored

show running-config | include aaa
aaa authentication enable default local
aaa authentication login default local
aaa authentication dot1x default group radius      <-- group A's, the auth-mac list is gone

show radius local-server user   ->   Total users: 4
  ckmulti1  ckmulti2  ckpeap  cktls                <-- group A's four; the MAC user is gone
```

🔴 **The full `show running-config` saved as `../swi_d.cfg` is BYTE-IDENTICAL to the ones saved
at the end of T33374 and T44277** (verified with `diff`): **zero config drift across three
cases**, including the two rig additions this case made and removed.

- No `write`; startup-config is still `flash:/default.cfg`. Boot image and config unchanged.
- No VLAN added or removed; vlan 60, vlan 10, vlan 48 untouched.
- `auth host-mode` was never touched in this case.
- Group A's rig (local RADIUS server, its four users, the NAS, the `local` trustpoint, the dot1x
  method list) left exactly as found.
- Nothing of ours left running on the box.

